WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.591
Risk management connects cybersecurity activity to mission and operational outcomes.

00:00:05.591 --> 00:00:12.155
Begin by defining the system, physical process, assets, dependencies, owners, and boundaries.

00:00:12.155 --> 00:00:22.122
Identify what the organization must protect: safe operation, reliable control, production, environmental limits, service delivery, data, and recovery capability.

00:00:22.122 --> 00:00:27.228
Next, consider threats, vulnerabilities, likelihood, existing safeguards, and impact.

00:00:27.228 --> 00:00:30.874
Threats may be malicious or accidental.

00:00:30.874 --> 00:00:35.493
Vulnerabilities may be technical, architectural, procedural, or human.

00:00:35.493 --> 00:00:44.002
Impact should include safety, equipment, quality, downtime, mission, environment, financial loss, and public consequence where applicable.

00:00:44.002 --> 00:00:48.135
Operations and engineering teams are essential participants.

00:00:48.135 --> 00:00:57.129
They understand process states, maintenance constraints, independent safety functions, and the consequences of losing visibility or control.

00:00:57.129 --> 00:01:04.180
Cybersecurity teams contribute threat knowledge, architecture analysis, access pathways, monitoring, and incident experience.

00:01:04.180 --> 00:01:09.285
Risk decisions are stronger when both perspectives are present.

00:01:09.285 --> 00:01:16.335
Control selection should reduce the most important risk without creating greater operational harm.

00:01:16.335 --> 00:01:21.440
Segmentation may reduce exposure while a patch awaits testing.

00:01:21.440 --> 00:01:27.031
Secure remote access may preserve vendor support while improving accountability.

00:01:27.031 --> 00:01:32.623
Backup and recovery controls may reduce the duration of disruption.

00:01:32.623 --> 00:01:36.755
Each decision should state assumptions and evidence.

00:01:36.755 --> 00:01:40.888
Residual risk remains after controls are applied.

00:01:40.888 --> 00:01:52.800
Accountable leadership must understand and accept that risk, fund additional treatment, avoid the activity, or transfer part of the exposure through appropriate arrangements.

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:58.878
Risk acceptance should not be an indefinite technical exception without ownership.

00:01:58.878 --> 00:02:04.956
Reassess when systems, connectivity, suppliers, threats, vulnerabilities, or process consequences change.

00:02:04.956 --> 00:02:12.492
Risk management is a continuing operational cycle, not a one-time document produced before commissioning.
